<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis XVI</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Louis XVI of France</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis XVI was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1774. He supported the American War of Independence, which worsened the crown's debts, and in 1789 he summoned the Estates-General, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. He accepted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in 1791 but was deposed in 1792, tried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> and guillotined in January 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xvi.jpg" alt="Louis XVI" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine-François Callet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine-Fran%C3%A7ois_Callet_-_Louis_XVI,_roi_de_France_et_de_Navarre_(1754-1793),_rev%C3%AAtu_du_grand_costume_royal_en_1779_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1787 the finance minister Calonne put a reform plan to an Assembly of Notables, which rejected it, and <b>Louis XVI</b> agreed to summon the Estates-General, which met at Versailles in May 1789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The income and taxes of slave-grown sugar became a major source of the French budget, and slave labour let France surpass Britain in trade; <b>Louis XVI</b> would not change the labour system even when economists showed free labour cheaper.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…escaped direct taxation, and debts from a century of wars, ending with the American War of Independence, forced <b>Louis XVI</b> to summon the Estates General, which met in May 1789; within weeks its commoners had proclaimed themselves a National Assembly and the absolute monarchy was at an end.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XV, who succeeded his great-grandfather Louis XIV at the age of five in 1715, and <b>Louis XVI</b>, from 1774, governed through royal councils, the controller-general of finances and the intendants of the provinces, with only about 10,000 officials for a kingdom of 28 million.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li><li><blockquote>It was sung mainly as a rallying cry and an entertainment of the revolutionaries, and it mocked King <b>Louis XVI</b>, Queen Marie Antoinette and the monarchists.</blockquote><a href="/songs/carmagnole">La Carmagnole</a></li><li><blockquote>When Prince Louis-Joseph, son of <b>Louis XVI</b> and Marie Antoinette and heir to the throne, was born in 1781, his wet nurse, a peasant named Geneviève Poitrine, sang it as she rocked the royal cradle.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li></ul></section>
